{"reference": "A", "saved_D_answer": "B", "saved_D_rationale": "Answer: B\nReason: The chest X-ray shows a widened mediastinum with an abnormal bulge of the left cardiac contour and a translucent line outlining the superior mediastinal border, which are classic radiographic signs of traumatic aortic injury (rupture of the ascending aorta or pulmonary trunk) following penetrating trauma. While pneumothorax is present, the specific mediastinal and cardiac contour abnormalities point to great vessel injury as the primary diagnosis described by these signs."}
